-- title:
--   Debian's libsodium 1.0.22-2 (arm64) crypto_onetimeauth and crypto_verify object code, as a placed library
-- statement:
--   The object code of libsodium's one-time-authentication functions and of `crypto_verify_16` as Debian ships it for 64-bit ARM, as one placed library in the `Arm64` semantics (google-deepmind/code-verification's `Arm64.Trusted`, republished as the `Def_Arm64_*` bundles). The four objects are `libsodium_la-crypto_onetimeauth.o`, `libsodium_la-onetimeauth_poly1305.o`, `libsodium_la-poly1305_donna.o` and `libsodium_la-verify.o`, taken unmodified from `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libsodium.a` in the Debian package `libsodium-dev_1.0.22-2_arm64.deb` (libsodium 1.0.22). Debian compiled them from `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/crypto_onetimeauth.c`, `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/onetimeauth_poly1305.c`, `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna.c` and `src/libsodium/crypto_verify/verify.c`. The first three objects are those of the bundle `Poly1305Debian_PlacedObj` of the companion mission; this bundle adds `verify.o`, so that `crypto_verify_16` is code here rather than an external function.
--
--   **Provenance.** The package's SHA-256 is `a78d13b5…8f3f`, as listed in Debian's `Packages` index for arm64 in unstable and testing, whose hash is in the `InRelease` files signed with Debian's archive keys. The objects' SHA-256s are `b75967a4…eda`, `c375c62d…4ee`, `3f97dc8c…2d5` and `dc150660…798a`. Every function of the four objects occurs byte for byte in the shared library `libsodium.so.26.4.0` of the package `libsodium26_1.0.22-2_arm64.deb`, apart from the words the linker fills in. `rust-obj2lean export` linked the four objects into this module. Its byte sidecar is inlined as literal lists, its imports are renamed to the published bundles, and its docstring's account of the upstream build is replaced (the file says so). DeepMind's exporter rejects ELF note sections, so we used a copy with a one-line change that skips them: [skip-note-sections.patch](https://github.com/dbenbenn/arm64-semantics-p2m/blob/159faf00becda47371f86336df32aa1c3e60bae4/tools/rust-obj2lean/skip-note-sections.patch), applied by the build script beside it. The objects' only note section is `.note.gnu.property`, which marks them as built with branch protection. No code reads it. The bundle's 2,324 code bytes are the four objects' `.text` sections, concatenated in order with their alignment padding.
--
--   **The library `libsodiumDebianOnetimeauthCtLib`.**
--   - `libsodiumDebianOnetimeauthCtText` is the code block: 34 function symbols with their offsets, the ten functions of `crypto_onetimeauth.c` from `0x0`, the ten of `onetimeauth_poly1305.c` from `0xa0`, the eight of `poly1305_donna.c` from `0x1a0` (each name carries the suffix “ [libsodium_la_poly1305_donna]”), and the six of `verify.c` from `0x720` (`crypto_verify_16_bytes`, `_32_bytes`, `_64_bytes`, `crypto_verify_16`, `crypto_verify_32`, `crypto_verify_64`). Gaps between the objects' sections are zero-filled.
--   - Each `crypto_onetimeauth*` function branches to its `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305*` counterpart. Each of those loads the pointer `implementation` and, through it, an entry of the table `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_implementation`, and branches there. The donna `_verify` computes the expected tag into a stack buffer (the compiler inlined the one-shot: the key setup in place, then calls of `poly1305_update` and `poly1305_finish`) and then calls `crypto_verify_16`, now a call inside the block.
--   - The external symbols are `__stack_chk_fail`, `memset`, `randombytes_buf` and `sodium_memzero`; the code also reads `__stack_chk_guard` through the global offset table. `poly1305_finish` calls `memset` and ends with a branch to `sodium_memzero`; the one-shot and `_verify` check the stack guard and call `__stack_chk_fail` if it changed; the key-generation functions branch to `randombytes_buf`.
--   - There are four data objects: the 8-byte writable pointer `implementation` (an `abs64` relocation to the table), the 40-byte writable table (five `abs64` relocations to the donna one-shot, `_verify`, `_init`, `_update` and `_final`), the read-only string `"poly1305"` with its terminating zero, and `optblocker_u16`, two zero bytes of `.bss`, writable, which `crypto_verify_n` reads as a `volatile`.
--   - The exports are `crypto_onetimeauth`, `crypto_onetimeauth_verify`, `crypto_onetimeauth_init`, `crypto_onetimeauth_update`, `crypto_onetimeauth_final`, `crypto_verify_16`, `crypto_verify_32` and `crypto_verify_64`: the functions the mission's statements are about.
--   - Debian builds with branch protection. Most functions begin with a `BTI` or `PACIASP` instruction, and those that save the link register authenticate it with `AUTIASP` before returning. These are hint instructions, and the `Arm64` model executes every hint instruction as a no-op.
--
--   **The source of `crypto_verify_16`.** `verify.c`, lines 60 and 62–83: “static volatile uint16_t optblocker_u16;”, and `crypto_verify_n`, which ORs the bytewise differences of the two inputs into a `volatile` accumulator over a loop of fixed length “for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { d |= x[i] ^ y[i]; }”, then computes “d--; d = ((d >> 13) ^ optblocker_u16) >> 2; return (int) d - 1;”; lines 87–103 define `crypto_verify_16`, `crypto_verify_32` and `crypto_verify_64` as `crypto_verify_n` with $n$ = 16, 32 and 64.
--
--   The statements quantify over every placement of this library that the `Arm64` model accepts (`Obj.PlacedLib.Placeable`), so no load address is fixed.
